  main/res/mipmap-xxxhdpi/ic_launcher_round.webp

THESE FILES ARE NOT IN THIS REPOSITORY. Their redistribution rights could not
be verified, so they were left out:

  - The two archival snapshots (experiments/test-a/ and experiments/test-b/)
    omit all 14 files. Each snapshot's OMITTED_ASSETS.md records the original
    file paths, byte sizes, SHA-256 checksums, Git blob IDs and the full source
    commit hash (77fe81a9f4aaa069ba39898f1812a98f7806c919 for Test A,
    1f95f80274552117509a047bd8f1981985df4be4 for Test B). The checksums
    identify the files; they do not reproduce them. The snapshots are therefore
    not byte-identical to the complete original commits.
  - The runnable root app/ uses an ORIGINAL launcher icon created for this
    project: a green square with a white and pale-green storage box, drawn as
    plain vector shapes. It replaces the four XML files above, and the ten
    .webp files were removed (minSdk is 26, so adaptive icons need no raster
    fallbacks). It contains no Google artwork or trademarks and is covered by
    the repository's Apache-2.0 license (LICENSE). Each of its four files
    carries a comment saying so.
  - The Kotlin code, the Gradle configuration and the archived benchmark
    results are unchanged. The icons are not drawn by the Compose previews.

Why the originals were removed. What was checked:

CONFIRMED (from the files and official pages):
  - The originals were present in the initial commit 0202cb3, before any
    experiment, and none carried a license or copyright header.
  - The xxxhdpi launcher image that was viewed showed the Android robot head
    on a green grid (#3DDC84).
  - Google's brand guidelines
    (https://developer.android.com/distribute/marketing-tools/brand-guidelines)
    say the green Android robot may be reproduced or modified with this
    attribution, and link CC BY 3.0:

      "The Android robot is reproduced or modified from work created and
      shared by Google and used according to terms described in the Creative
      Commons 3.0 Attribution License."

  - Google's Legal Notice (https://developer.android.com/legal) says the robot
    "can be used, reproduced, and modified freely in marketing communications"
    and is licensed under Creative Commons Attribution, but it links version
    2.5. The two official pages therefore disagree on the version, and
    neither says whether the robot may be shipped inside an app's source
    repository.
  - Android Studio's terms (https://developer.android.com/studio/terms) say
    nothing about ownership of, or a license to, templates or generated
    assets, and restrict redistribution of SDK parts except under the terms of
    open-source components (sections 3.4 and 3.5). developer.android.com's
    create-app-icons page says nothing about the terms of the default icon.

INFERRED (not confirmed):
  - That the originals are the default launcher icon of an Android Studio
    "new project" template. The .idea files in commit 0202cb3 show the project
    was opened in Android Studio, but the generating tool is not recorded, and
    no official source was found to match these files byte for byte.
  - That the other density variants and the vector files show the same
    artwork. Only the xxxhdpi image was viewed.

Trademark rights (separate from copyright licensing):
  Google keeps its trademark rights in the Android robot logo and in the
  Android name. The brand guidelines state that you may not file trademark
  applications for, or claim trademark rights to, the Android robot or its
  derivatives. The Legal Notice also bars use of the Android logo and its
  typeface, and developer.android.com/license says Google trademarks,
  including the stylized Android logo, are not covered by the Apache 2.0 or
  Creative Commons licenses it describes. A copyright license for artwork does
  not grant any trademark right, and nothing in this repository suggests
  endorsement by Google. The replacement icon is not a derivative of the robot.


